Format: https://www.debian.org/doc/packaging-manuals/copyright-format/1.0/
Upstream-Name: linscp
Upstream-Contact: Eduardo Correia <ecorreia@apliant.com.br>
Source: https://github.com/99ecarvalho/linscp
Comment:
 The upstream release tarball (linscp-X.Y.Z.tar.xz) is built by
 port/tools/make_source_tarball.py from the linscp repository and the
 submodules it pins: 3rd-party/winscp (the core linscp builds on),
 3rd-party/putty (stock PuTTY, read only by the test suite) and
 3rd-party/openssl (an OpenSSL release, not used by this package).
 .
 Embedded code copies (Debian Policy 4.13). The package links the system
 OpenSSL and expat (-DLINSCP_SYSTEM_OPENSSL=ON -DLINSCP_SYSTEM_EXPAT=ON); the
 bundled copies of both stay in the source but are not compiled. Three
 copies are compiled, and cannot be replaced by Debian's packages:
  - PuTTY (3rd-party/winscp/source/putty, based on PuTTY 0.85): PuTTY is a
    set of programs, not a library; the core builds its SSH, SFTP and SCP
    protocol code into linscp, with changes that adapt it to the core's
    session and configuration layers.
  - neon (3rd-party/winscp/libs/neon, based on neon 0.37.1): the core uses
    API, session flags and structure layouts that only the modified copy
    has (TLS session reuse, certificate storage, a separate "real host" for
    S3 virtual-host endpoints, larger buffers and SHA-256 fingerprints).
    Debian's libneon27 is 0.34 and has none of it.
  - libs3 (3rd-party/winscp/libs/libs3, from libs3 git master): its HTTP
    layer is rewritten to run over neon instead of libcurl, and its API is
    extended (session, TLS and response-data callbacks, requester-pays,
    per-request multipart contexts). Debian's libs3 (2.0) offers none of it.
 They are to be listed in the security tracker's embedded-code-copies
 file. The changes are described in doc/qt/bundled-libraries.md and
 doc/qt/putty-deltas.md.

